ACE-Inhibitor-Induced Angioedema.
  
### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient K.V.
* Age: 61 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 82 kg
* Medical History: Essential Hypertension (3 years), hyperlipidemia, no history of food or environmental allergies.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Initiated on Enalapril 10 mg orally once daily for blood pressure management.
* Month 4 (Day 12): Patient woke up with acute, painless swelling of the lower lip, tongue, and soft palate, accompanied by mild voice hoarseness and difficulty swallowing (dysphagia).
* Laboratory and Clinical Findings at Admission:
  * Soft tissue edema of the labial and lingual structures without urticaria or pruritus.
  * Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy: Moderate supraglottic edema without complete airway obstruction.
  * C1 Esterase Inhibitor Level: 28 mg/dL (Normal reference range: 21 to 39 mg/dL; rules out hereditary angioedema).
  * Serum Tryptase: 4.1 ng/mL (Normal reference range: under 11.4 ng/mL; rules out IgE-mediated anaphylaxis).
* Intervention: Immediate permanent cessation of Enalapril; patient admitted to the step-down unit for continuous airway monitoring, IV methylprednisolone, and IM epinephrine on standby.
* Follow-up (Day 3 post-discontinuation): Complete resolution of mucosal swelling and dysphagia; transitioned to Amlodipine 5 mg daily for hypertension.

